OpenChoice
==========

OpenChoice is a derivative work. It is an independent, embeddable
reimplementation of the model/hardware fit problem first solved by:

    llmfit — https://github.com/AlexsJones/llmfit
    Copyright (c) 2026 Alex Jones
    MIT License

Both projects are MIT licensed, and the upstream copyright notice is retained
in this repository's LICENSE file as that license requires.

What was taken
--------------

OpenChoice is not a fork of the llmfit source tree; no llmfit source file is
copied into this repository. What it does carry forward is the reasoning,
which is the genuinely hard-won part of that project:

  * The idea that a fit verdict should be a pure function of memory-pool
    utilisation, capped by what the execution path can deliver, rather than a
    blend of several overlapping signals.
  * The verdict bands themselves (60% / 85% / 98%) and the reasoning for
    stopping the top band short of 100%.
  * The observation that decode is memory-bandwidth-bound and prefill is
    compute-bound, and that the two must be estimated separately — including
    reporting an unknown prefill as null rather than zero.
  * The bandwidth-roofline decode formula and its 0.55 efficiency derate.
  * Walking a quantization hierarchy to find the best format that fits,
    instead of assuming one.
  * Treating usable context as distinct from a model's advertised window.
  * Per-use-case scoring weights across quality, speed, fit, and context.

The GPU bandwidth and fp16 throughput figures in `openchoice-core` were
compiled independently from vendor specifications.

What is new here
----------------

  * The `.ocb` packed catalog format and its zero-copy, allocation-free
    reader, which is what makes the catalog fit in microcontroller flash.
  * A `no_std`, dependency-free, allocation-free engine (`openchoice-core`)
    that builds for bare-metal RISC-V and Xtensa targets.
  * The device console (`openchoice-embedded`) and ESP32 firmware.
  * Recalibrated capability scoring, cross-domain alignment penalties, and
    collapsing of repackaged duplicates in the ranking.

If you want the full-featured desktop tool — the TUI, the web dashboard, the
REST API, community benchmark sharing, the Kubernetes DRA integration — use
llmfit. It does considerably more than this does. OpenChoice exists to answer
the same question in 57 KB of code on a device with no operating system.
